But we need to combine both conditions: maybe use ( (\old(this.get(i)) == null) => \result[i] == null ) && ( (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => \result[i].equals(\old(this.get(i))) ). Since both are per i, we need to put them inside forall: \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (\old(this.get(i)) == null) => \result[i] == null ) && ( (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => \result[i].equals(\old(this.get(i))) );

Alternatively we could use Objects.equals(\result[i], \old(this.get(i))) which captures both null and non-null equality. That would be simpler: assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < \old(this.size()); java.util.Objects.equals(\result[i], \old(this.get(i)));

But the spec may not allow static method calls? Probably allowed. But earlier examples used .equals method. We'll follow pattern: use .equals for non-null case.

